  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 330 AM MDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nMonday will be another hot day across the area, though daily highs \nshould be 1-3 degrees cooler than Sunday. The persistent, powerful \nridge aloft will shift slightly eastward of its weekend position \nover the Four Corners, opening up the tap for another surge of \nmonsoon moisture into our area. NAEFS mean precipitable water climbs \nback to the climatological 90th percentile by late Monday and \nremains there through at least Wednesday. A shortwave trough will \nalso push through late in the day on Monday, enhancing lift across \nthe area. As a result, we can expect to see an increase in shower \nand thunderstorm coverage on Monday, Tuesday, and possibly \nWednesday. In addition, we will see an increase in the potential for \nthese storms to produce appreciable rainfall beyond just gusty \nwinds. Temperatures are expected to remain warm (particularly \novernight), but we will be closer to seasonal averages Tuesday \nonwards compared to the weekend heatwave.\n\nEnsembles show a gradual decrease in precipitable water Thursday \nonward as the upper level ridge very slowly retrogrades back to the \nwest. Towards the end of the forecast period, the ridge position is \nin a much more unfavorable position for potent monsoon surges like \nwe saw over the last few days, but ample recycled moisture (and \nperhaps stronger forcing than a typical monsoon surge) should keep \ndaily chances for scattered showers and thunderstorms in the \nforecast.",
